# Neuron — local configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` beside it and edit that copy. `.env` is gitignored
# and must never be committed; this file IS committed and must therefore never
# hold a real secret. Everything below is a placeholder.
#
# A real environment variable always beats a value set here. That is what lets
# the same code run in both places: on a laptop this file supplies the values,
# while in production no `.env` file is shipped at all and the hosting platform
# sets real environment variables instead.
#
# `PORT` and `DATABASE_PATH` are optional and the values below are also their
# defaults, so copying those two changes nothing. **`JWT_SECRET` is required and
# the placeholder below will not work** — a straight copy of this file no longer
# boots, deliberately, and the last section says how to generate one.
#
# A variable that is set but unusable stops the API from starting, with a message
# naming the variable and quoting the value. See
# docs/decisions/ADR-007-configuration-and-boot-validation.md.

# The TCP port the API listens on.
# Must be a whole number between 1 and 65535. Anything else — empty, 0,
# negative, above the range, text, or a number with stray spaces around it —
# refuses to boot rather than being quietly corrected.
# Remove the line entirely to use the default, 3000.
PORT=3000

# Which SQLite file holds the journal.
# A relative path is resolved from the directory the API process was started
# in, which is `apps/api` for `pnpm dev` — so the value below names exactly the
# same file as the default, `apps/api/data/neuron.db`.
# Remove the line entirely to use that default.
# If the file named here does not exist, the API warns loudly and then creates
# an empty one, so a mistyped path shows up as a warning rather than as a
# journal that appears to have lost every entry.
DATABASE_PATH=data/neuron.db

# The key that signs every access token. REQUIRED — there is no default and no
# fallback, and the API refuses to start without it.
#
# The value below is a placeholder and must be replaced. It is the right length
# and it is in version control, which is exactly what makes it useless: anyone
# who can read this repository could use it to forge a token for any user. That
# is the reason there is no default in the code either — a built-in secret looks
# configured while protecting nothing.
#
# Generate your own, once, and keep it out of git:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Must be at least 32 characters. That is a length floor rather than a strength
# test — no check can measure the entropy of a single string — and what it rules
# out is the failure that actually happens: a short word copied from a tutorial.
#
# Changing it invalidates every token already issued, so every signed-in client
# is logged out. See docs/decisions/ADR-012-authentication-endpoints.md.
JWT_SECRET=replace-this-placeholder-with-a-generated-value
